-- title:
--   Row and column projections of matrix functions
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Sign matrices are Boolean $m\times m$ matrices $S$; $C_{k,l}(f)$ is the total Fourier coefficient of $f$ over $k\times l$ rectangles, and $T_j$ denotes the Chebyshev polynomial of degree $j$. Let $F$ be a function on sign matrices.
--
--   1. **Row projection.**
--   $$
--   (\mathcal R_iF)(S)=\mathbb E_\sigma\,\sigma_i\,F(\mathrm{row}(\sigma)\oplus S),
--   $$
--   which keeps the part of $F$ whose frequencies meet row $i$ in an odd way.
--   2. **Column projection.** $(\mathcal C_jF)(S)=\mathbb E_\tau\,\tau_j\,F(\mathrm{col}(\tau)\oplus S)$, the analogous operation for column $j$.
